examples.question_text:
Use Prim's algorithm starting at $A$ to construct a minimum spanning tree and calculate its total weight. Explain why the second edge selected is $AD$ rather than $BD$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (1.6,1.4);
\coordinate (C) at (5,2.4);
\coordinate (D) at (5,0);
\coordinate (E) at (7,-1.4);
\coordinate (F) at (5,-2.6);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,above left] {$3$};
\draw (A)--(D) node[midway,above] {$6$};
\draw (A)--(F) node[midway,below left] {$7$};
\draw (B)--(C) node[midway,above] {$9$};
\draw (B)--(D) node[midway,above] {$7$};
\draw (C)--(D) node[midway,left] {$4$};
\draw (C)--(E) node[midway,right] {$6$};
\draw (D)--(E) node[midway,above right] {$7$};
\draw (D)--(F) node[midway,left] {$8$};
\draw (E)--(F) node[midway,below right] {$3$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/left,B/above left,C/above,D/right,E/right,F/below}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Start at $A$. At each step, compare edges from all vertices already in the tree to vertices outside it.

| Vertices currently in the tree | Edge added | Weight |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $A$ | $AB$ | $3$ |
| $A,B$ | $AD$ | $6$ |
| $A,B,D$ | $DC$ | $4$ |
| $A,B,C,D$ | $CE$ | $6$ |
| $A,B,C,D,E$ | $EF$ | $3$ |

After adding $AB$, choose $AD=6$ rather than $BD=7$: both reach an unvisited vertex, and $AD$ has the lower weight.

The tree connects all six vertices with five edges and no cycles.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (1.6,1.4);
\coordinate (C) at (5,2.4);
\coordinate (D) at (5,0);
\coordinate (E) at (7,-1.4);
\coordinate (F) at (5,-2.6);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,above left] {$3$};
\draw (A)--(D) node[midway,above] {$6$};
\draw (D)--(C) node[midway,left] {$4$};
\draw (C)--(E) node[midway,right] {$6$};
\draw (E)--(F) node[midway,below right] {$3$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/left,B/above left,C/above,D/right,E/right,F/below}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}\text{Total weight}&=3+6+4+6+3\\&=22\end{aligned}$

Draw one edge for each direct road; the repeated table entries describe the same road.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2.6);
\coordinate (B) at (4,2.6);
\coordinate (C) at (4,0);
\coordinate (D) at (0,0);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,above] {$6$};
\draw (B)--(C) node[midway,right] {$4$};
\draw (C)--(D) node[midway,below] {$5$};
\draw (D)--(A) node[midway,left] {$9$};
\draw (A)--(C) node[midway,above right] {$7$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/above left,B/above right,C/below right,D/below left}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Start at $A$ and choose $AB=6$. From $A,B$, choose $BC=4$. From $A,B,C$, choose $CD=5$ rather than $AD=9$.

The minimum spanning tree is:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2.6);
\coordinate (B) at (4,2.6);
\coordinate (C) at (4,0);
\coordinate (D) at (0,0);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,above] {$6$};
\draw (B)--(C) node[midway,right] {$4$};
\draw (C)--(D) node[midway,below] {$5$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/above left,B/above right,C/below right,D/below left}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}\text{Total time}&=6+4+5\\&=15\text{ minutes}\end{aligned}$

examples.question_text:
The minimum spanning tree of this network includes the edge labelled $k$ and has total weight $27$. Find $k$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,1.5);
\coordinate (B) at (2,3);
\coordinate (C) at (2,0);
\coordinate (D) at (4.5,2);
\coordinate (E) at (6,1.2);
\coordinate (F) at (6.7,3);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,above left] {$5$};
\draw (A)--(C) node[midway,below left] {$4$};
\draw (B)--(D) node[midway,above] {$7$};
\draw (C)--(D) node[midway,below right] {$6$};
\draw (D)--(E) node[midway,below left] {$4$};
\draw (D)--(F) node[midway,above left] {$k$};
\draw (E)--(F) node[midway,right] {$9$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/left,B/above,C/below,D/above,E/below,F/above}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The edge $DF$ belongs to the MST. Begin with that edge and compare edges from both its vertices to unvisited vertices. Add $DE=4$, $DC=6$, $CA=4$, then $AB=5$.

$\begin{aligned}k+4+6+4+5&=27\\k+19&=27\\k&=8\end{aligned}$

Check with $k=8$: Prim's algorithm starting at $A$ selects $AC=4$, $AB=5$, $CD=6$, $DE=4$, then $DF=8$ rather than $EF=9$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,1.5);
\coordinate (B) at (2,3);
\coordinate (C) at (2,0);
\coordinate (D) at (4.5,2);
\coordinate (E) at (6,1.2);
\coordinate (F) at (6.7,3);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,above left] {$5$};
\draw (A)--(C) node[midway,below left] {$4$};
\draw (C)--(D) node[midway,below right] {$6$};
\draw (D)--(E) node[midway,below left] {$4$};
\draw (D)--(F) node[midway,above left] {$8$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/left,B/above,C/below,D/above,E/below,F/above}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\text{Total weight}=4+5+6+4+8=27$.

examples.question_text:
The minimum spanning tree of this network includes the edges labelled $a$ and $b$ and has total weight $30$. Which candidate pair gives their values?

Candidates: $a=7,b=6$; $a=9,b=5$; $a=6,b=8$; $a=8,b=8$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,3);
\coordinate (B) at (6,3);
\coordinate (C) at (0,0);
\coordinate (D) at (6,0);
\coordinate (E) at (2,2);
\coordinate (F) at (4,1);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,above] {$12$};
\draw (A)--(C) node[midway,left] {$9$};
\draw (C)--(D) node[midway,below] {$11$};
\draw (B)--(D) node[midway,right] {$b$};
\draw (A)--(E) node[midway,above right] {$4$};
\draw (C)--(E) node[midway,above left] {$7$};
\draw (E)--(F) node[midway,above right] {$a$};
\draw (F)--(B) node[midway,above left] {$6$};
\draw (F)--(D) node[midway,below left] {$9$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/above left,B/above right,C/below left,D/below right,E/above,F/below}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Test each candidate using Prim's algorithm from $A$. When lowest weights tie, either eligible edge may be selected.

| $a$ | $b$ | Edges added in order | Total weight |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| $7$ | $6$ | $AE,EF,FB,BD,EC$ | $4+7+6+6+7=30$ |
| $9$ | $5$ | $AE,EC,EF,FB,BD$ | $4+7+9+6+5=31$ |
| $6$ | $8$ | $AE,EF,FB,EC,BD$ | $4+6+6+7+8=31$ |
| $8$ | $8$ | $AE,EC,EF,FB,BD$ | $4+7+8+6+8=33$ |

Only $a=7$ and $b=6$ give the required total. The corresponding minimum spanning tree is:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,3);
\coordinate (B) at (6,3);
\coordinate (C) at (0,0);
\coordinate (D) at (6,0);
\coordinate (E) at (2,2);
\coordinate (F) at (4,1);
\draw (A)--(E) node[midway,above right] {$4$};
\draw (C)--(E) node[midway,above left] {$7$};
\draw (E)--(F) node[midway,above right] {$7$};
\draw (F)--(B) node[midway,above left] {$6$};
\draw (B)--(D) node[midway,right] {$6$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/above left,B/above right,C/below left,D/below right,E/above,F/below}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
